{% block description %}Sagamok is moving ahead with an enforcement by-law before members have seen the new membership law that will decide who counts as a member.{% endblock %}
{% block og_title %}Membership should come before trespass{% endblock %}
{% block og_description %}One law decides who belongs. The other decides who may be removed. Members have been shown the second law first.{% endblock %}

{% block article_body %}
  <p class="lead">Sagamok members were shown what happens if you are not a member before they were shown the new rules that will decide who counts as a member.</p>

  <p>Sagamok already has a Membership Law. It was passed on May 26, 1999 and still governs who is on Sagamok's membership list. Nothing has replaced it.</p>

  <p>Sagamok is now starting work on a replacement. A Membership Law development gathering is scheduled for August 11 and 12. No replacement draft has been released.</p>

  <p>The Trespass By-law is further ahead. Its draft is dated July 21 and its engagement session is July 28. The draft is posted on Sagamok's <a href="https://www.sagamokanishnawbek.com/general-documents">General Documents page</a>, a members-only area that requires a login.</p>

  <div class="news-article__plain">
    <strong>The order matters</strong>
    <p>Membership rules decide who counts as a member. The Trespass By-law uses that status to decide who must prove a right to remain. Members have seen the enforcement draft first.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Why the Trespass draft depends on membership</h2>

  <p>The connection is written directly into the draft.</p>

  <p>Section 2 defines a member as a person whose name appears, or who is entitled to have their name appear, on the Sagamok Membership list. Under section 10, a non-member found on Sagamok lands starts from the presumption that they are a trespasser and must show otherwise.</p>

  <div class="news-article__plain">
    <strong>What section 10 does</strong>
    <p>The draft treats a non-member found on Sagamok lands as a presumed trespasser unless they prove otherwise.</p>
  </div>

  <p>The draft applies to everyone. Council could temporarily or permanently exclude an individual, including a member. It also permits conditions on entry, demands for identifying information, removal, arrest without warrant in stated circumstances, discontinuation of Sagamok services and final appeals to a committee appointed by Council.</p>

  <p>Those powers have already been put into draft form. The future membership rules have not.</p>

  <h2>What the current Membership Law does</h2>

  <p>The 1999 Membership Law does more than keep a list. It creates an elected Membership Authority. It sets eligibility and application rules, permits probation for up to five years, provides protest and appeal processes, and reserves expulsion for a three-quarter vote at a general membership meeting.</p>

  <p>It also says a person who renounces membership or is expelled must dispose of land and improvements within one year. Amendments require a majority of members present at a properly called general membership meeting.</p>

  <p>Those are member-controlled safeguards. The August gathering notice does not say whether the replacement law will preserve them.</p>

  <h2>Sagamok has two different membership records</h2>

  <p>Sagamok controls its Band List under section 10 of the Indian Act. Federal registration and membership under Sagamok's own law are related, but they are not the same decision.</p>

  <p>Council minutes dated May 19, 2026 describe approximately 399 more people on the broader Indigenous Services Canada registry than on the Sagamok Membership Law list.</p>

  <p>Which list applies can affect who votes and who takes part in community decisions. Under the First Nations Elections Act, Band List status is part of eligibility to vote in Council elections. Sagamok also used its Membership Law list to decide who could vote in a 2024 settlement poll, while allowing the broader group with Sagamok registry numbers to receive the distribution.</p>

  <p>The replacement Membership Law could narrow the difference, preserve it or change the rules another way. No draft has been released, so none of those outcomes can be assumed.</p>

  <h2>Who decides membership during enforcement?</h2>

  <p>The Trespass draft does not lock its definition of member to the 1999 law or to a membership list dated on the day the by-law is enacted. It refers generally to the Sagamok Membership list.</p>

  <p>That means a later Membership Law could change who falls into the Trespass By-law's non-member category without changing the Trespass text.</p>

  <p>The phrase "entitled to have their name appear" also needs an operating rule. Someone could have a membership application pending, be under probation, dispute a deletion or have federal registration while remaining outside Sagamok's local list. The draft does not explain who resolves that question at the roadside, at a home or during removal.</p>

  <h2>The two processes collect some of the same information</h2>

  <p>The Membership Law gathering will discuss kinship, adoption, community acceptance, identity, belonging and Elders' oral histories. The event package says the gathering will be recorded and that information may be used in curriculum and community-history work.</p>

  <p>The Trespass draft separately defines identifying information to include family name and lineage history, children's names, parents, grandparents, birth date and Indian Registration number. It permits collection through demands, observation, photography, video and CCTV, with disclosure in stated circumstances.</p>

  <p>One process collects information to decide belonging. The other collects information that can be used during enforcement. There is no evidence that the two information systems will be connected. Members need to know whether information collected in one process can be used in the other.</p>

  <section class="news-article__questions">
    <h2>Six answers are needed before the Trespass By-law is passed</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Which Membership Law and membership list will control when the Trespass By-law takes effect?</li>
      <li>How will people registered with Indigenous Services Canada but not on Sagamok's membership list be treated?</li>
      <li>What happens to pending applicants and probationary members?</li>
      <li>Who decides whether someone is entitled to appear on the list during an enforcement encounter?</li>
      <li>Will enforcement stop while disputed membership status is reviewed or appealed?</li>
      <li>Can Council or enforcement officers access membership, family or genealogy records?</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <h2>What the record shows</h2>

  <p>The chronology does not prove that either law was designed to exclude particular people. It does not establish a linked database or an improper purpose.</p>

  <p>It establishes something simpler: the Trespass By-law is already drafted while the replacement Membership Law has not been released.</p>

  <p>The new Membership Law draft, a comparison with the 1999 law, the rules for moving from one law to the other and the approval process should be released before the Trespass By-law is passed. Members need to know who the two laws will treat as a member, a non-member and a person entitled to remain.</p>
